One important point: some features available in Java 21 were introduced in earlier versions. I'll mark those clearly so you don't accidentally claim they were introduced in Java 21.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Virtual Threads — Java 21
============================
Problem before: Traditional platform threads are relatively expensive when an application handles many concurrent blocking tasks.

Before Java 21:

Runnable task = () -> {
    System.out.println("Processing request");
};

Thread thread = new Thread(task);
thread.start();

With Java 21:

Thread.startVirtualThread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Processing request");
});

You can also create a virtual-thread-per-task executor:

try (var executor =
         java.util.concurrent.Executors
             .newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {

    executor.submit(() -> {
        System.out.println("Processing request");
    });
}

Why useful? Virtual threads make it easier to handle large numbers of concurrent, mostly blocking tasks, such as HTTP requests or database calls. They do not automatically make CPU-intensive tasks faster.

Interview answer: “Virtual threads are lightweight threads introduced in Java 21 to improve scalability for applications with many concurrent blocking operations.”

2. Pattern Matching for switch — Final in Java 21
===================================================
Before: We often used instanceof, explicit casts, and multiple conditions.

static String describe(Object obj) {
    if (obj instanceof String) {
        String value = (String) obj;
        return "String: " + value;
    }

    if (obj instanceof Integer) {
        Integer value = (Integer) obj;
        return "Integer: " + value;
    }

    return "Unknown";
}

After:

static String describe(Object obj) {
    return switch (obj) {
        case String value -> "String: " + value;
        case Integer value -> "Integer: " + value;
        case null -> "Null value";
        default -> "Unknown";
    };
}

Why useful? Cleaner type-based branching, less casting, and better readability.

Interview answer: “Pattern matching for switch lets us select a branch based on an object's type and bind it to a variable directly in the case.”

Note: this feature was previewed before Java 21 and became final in Java 21.

3. Record Patterns — Java 21
================================
Problem before: If a record contains data, we need to extract its values using accessor methods.

record Employee(String name, int age) {}

static void printEmployee(Object obj) {
    if (obj instanceof Employee) {
        Employee e = (Employee) obj;
        System.out.println(e.name());
        System.out.println(e.age());
    }
}

After:

record Employee(String name, int age) {}

static void printEmployee(Object obj) {
    if (obj instanceof Employee(String name, int age)) {
        System.out.println(name);
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}

Why useful? Record patterns let you check a record's type and extract its components in one step.

Interview answer: “Record patterns, finalized in Java 21, simplify extracting component values from records and support nested patterns.” 
