4. Sequenced Collections — Java 21
===================================
Problem before: Collections had different ways to access their first and last elements. For example, List had get(0) and get(size - 1).

Before Java 21:

List<String> names =
        new ArrayList<>(List.of("Amit", "Riya", "Neha"));

String first = names.get(0);
String last = names.get(names.size() - 1);

After Java 21:

List<String> names =
        new ArrayList<>(List.of("Amit", "Riya", "Neha"));

String first = names.getFirst();
String last = names.getLast();

names.addFirst("Rahul");
names.addLast("Priya");

List<String> reversed = names.reversed();

Why useful? A consistent API for working with the first and last elements of ordered collections.

Interview answer: “Java 21 introduced sequenced collection interfaces and methods such as getFirst(), getLast(), and reversed().”

Remember: reversed() provides a reverse-ordered view; it does not necessarily create a separate reversed copy. 
