9. Key Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM) API — Java 21
==================================================
What problem does it solve? Secure systems sometimes need to establish a shared secret key using public-key cryptography.

Java 21 introduced a KEM API for this purpose.

A simplified view of the workflow:

// Conceptual workflow, not complete executable code

// 1. Receiver generates a public/private key pair.
// 2. Sender encapsulates a shared secret using the public key.
// 3. Receiver decapsulates it using the private key.
// 4. Both sides derive the same shared secret.

Why useful? It provides a standard Java API for key encapsulation, useful in cryptographic protocols and secure communication.

Interview answer: “The KEM API, introduced in Java 21, supports establishing shared secret keys using public-key cryptography.”